---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import KeyRotationChecklist from '../../components/KeyRotationChecklist.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="API Key Rotation Checklist" description="An interactive checklist for safely rotating API keys without downtime.">
  <KeyRotationChecklist client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why rotation is riskier than generating a new key</h2>
  <p>Generating a new key is trivial — the actual risk is in the cutover, where every environment and integration using the old key must be updated before it's revoked, or you'll cause an outage for anything still relying on the old credential.</p>

  <h2>How to use this checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Work through each phase in order: before, during, and after rotation.</li>
    <li>Don't revoke the old key until you've confirmed every consumer has switched to the new one.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Revoking the old key immediately</strong> after issuing a new one, before confirming every service/environment has actually been updated.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting a key is used in CI/CD secrets</strong> or a less-visible integration, causing an unexpected failure after rotation.</li>
    <li><strong>Not scheduling rotation during low-traffic hours</strong> when the service can't support zero-downtime dual-key support.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Planning a routine scheduled key rotation for security hygiene</li>
    <li>Rotating a key urgently after a suspected leak</li>
    <li>Establishing a repeatable rotation process for a team</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How often should API keys be rotated?</strong> There's no universal answer — it depends on your risk tolerance and any compliance requirements, but having a repeatable, low-risk process (like this checklist) makes more frequent rotation practical.</p>
</ToolLayout>
